00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:36.220
And we're lucky to hear from
nine of them in this session.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.695
And so, speakers, you have
five minutes for your talk.

00:00:38.695 --> 00:00:40.320
And we're on a bit
of a tight schedule.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:44.880
I'll show you this one-minute
when there's one minute left.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.182
And there are two
options for mics.

00:00:47.182 --> 00:00:49.140
If you want to use the
handheld mic, feel free.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.440
If you want the clip
mic, then [? Omeed ?]

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.440
will help you with that.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:55.230
So let's start with--

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.902
we have Sorrachai
from the algorithms--

00:00:57.902 --> 00:00:59.985
from the data structures
and optimization program.

00:01:02.318 --> 00:01:03.860
SORRACHAI
YINGCHAREONTHAWORNCHAI: OK,

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:05.960
thank you for the introduction.

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:09.290
So this first lightning talk
is about vertex connectivity

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:11.420
in poly-logarithmic max-flows.

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.480
This is joint work with
Jason Lee, Danupon Nanongkai,

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.540
Debmalya Panigrahi, and
Thatchaphol Saranurak.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:20.840
I want to spend for
three to four minutes

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.600
to explain why this is
an interesting result.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.910
And, first of all,
let me tell you

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:28.100
that the equivalent
title is also

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:30.500
just something like vertex
connectivity in almost

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:31.250
linear time.

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:32.990
Because we know
that max-flow can

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:37.490
be solved in almost linear time
by the breakthrough result.

00:01:37.490 --> 00:01:39.380
So let me first
start by defining

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:41.180
what the [INAUDIBLE]
vertex connectivity.

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:43.910
Basically, I give you a
graph, undirected graph,

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:45.590
with n nodes and n edges.

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:48.440
I want to compute the
minimum number of vertices

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.400
such that, when I remove,
I disconnect the graph.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.250
For example, in this
particular graph,

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:56.210
the vertex connectivity is two
because I can remove these two

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:59.090
node to disconnect the graph,
but I cannot remove only

00:01:59.090 --> 00:02:01.790
a single node to
disconnect the graph.

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:05.900
And I would like to also mention
a very related problem called

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:07.370
the st-vertex mincut.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:11.300
So, in addition, you are
given also two nodes, s and t.

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:13.710
And then, the goal is
to compute kappa s, t,

00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:16.010
which is the minimum
number of vertices whose

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.290
removal disconnects s and t.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:23.660
So how this is a problem
related to the original problem?

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:24.990
I will tell you in a second.

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:28.820
But let me explain that
this s, t vertex mincut

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:31.850
problem is very important.

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:35.630
So one important fact is
that computing this number

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:38.438
can be computed by a max-flow.

00:02:38.438 --> 00:02:39.980
You can run by the
max-flow algorithm

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:40.938
to compute this number.

00:02:40.938 --> 00:02:43.452
This follow because of the
max-flow min-cut theorem,

00:02:43.452 --> 00:02:45.327
saying that maximum
number vertex disjoint s,

00:02:45.327 --> 00:02:49.280
t path is the same as the
minimum cut separating s and t.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:52.160
So, in this example,
for example,

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:53.960
here the minimum
vertex cut is two,

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:58.070
but the maximum flow, the
vertex capacitated, is also two.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:02.000
And one simple observation
is that you can--

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.690
vertex connectivity,
you can compute

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:08.287
by just minimum of all
pair possible s and t

00:03:08.287 --> 00:03:09.370
and then compute max-flow.

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:11.340
So, basically, you can
compute the connectivity

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.720
by running all pair max-flow.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:16.620
So that's one simple algorithm.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.640
Then, let me give you a very
quick survey of what is known.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.230
So this problem is a very
well-studied problem.

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.540
So it has a really long history.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:29.670
And the earliest algorithm
is done by n square cores

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:30.600
of maximum flow time.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.100
And there are a lot
of work that are

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:36.150
trying to reduce number of
cores of the number of flows.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:39.330
And the result by
Becker et al in '82,

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:41.400
they managed to reduce
to n cores of max-flow

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.740
to solve this vertex
connectivity problem.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:45.180
And the state of
the art algorithm

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.450
is either by linear
lower regression,

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:52.830
or by using algebraic technique,
or using a preflow algorithm

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:54.520
by Henzinger and Rao and Gabow.

00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:58.770
And one of the central
question in this area

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:00.510
is that whether or
not we can solve

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:03.210
this problem in linear time.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:06.950
And this has been conjectured
by Aho, Hopcrof, and Ullman

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:09.410
very long ago in 1974.

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:12.260
And we only know
the recent progress

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:13.790
for small connectivity.

00:04:13.790 --> 00:04:16.790
We know how to do
that in linear time

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:20.540
when connectivity is very small.

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:22.910
And what is the main point?

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:26.960
The main result of this
work is, basically, we

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:30.530
can solve this problem using
instead of n causal max-flow,

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:32.840
we can solve in
poly-logarithmic max-flow.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:34.670
And using the
recent breakthrough

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:38.000
of max-flow algorithm, we
can solve the whole problem

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.930
in almost linear time.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:43.850
And I just want to
highlight the fact

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:45.740
that the tools that
we use that are

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:48.890
different from the previous work
are based on something called

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:51.950
a local computation and
also sublinear algorithm

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:52.760
that we use.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:55.880
And I just want to
highlight some techniques

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:59.750
that we use here include
isolating cut lemma, sublinear

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:03.170
time kernelization, some
sketching, and sparse recovery.

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:05.960
So we use a lot of modern
tools to solve this problem.

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:09.620
But one thing that I
would like to highlight

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:15.050
on what is the main difference
from this approach compared

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:17.120
to the previous approach
is the following,

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:23.190
that in the previous approach,
they try to give you a graph,

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:26.280
and then they try to generate
many instances of max-flow.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.770
And each instance
has size linear m.

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:31.030
So the total size
of max-flow instance

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:35.940
would be something like the
number of core max-flow times

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:36.480
m.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:37.920
And then, the goal
was to minimize

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:39.300
the number of core max-flow.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:41.520
And the best known you
can do is just linear.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:44.190
But what we do here
differently is the following.

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:48.180
That instead of generating
a max-flow of size m,

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:51.490
we generate many, many max-flow
instance of smaller size.

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:55.110
So, in particular, each of these
instances can be sublinear.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:57.055
But the goal here,
we are not trying

00:05:57.055 --> 00:05:58.680
to minimize the number
of cores, but we

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:01.510
minimize the size-- total size
of the max-flow instances.

00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:03.390
So instead of you get--

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:06.660
we just generate
smaller instances such

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:09.840
that the total instance
is still a overall when

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.855
you sum total is still linear.

00:06:11.855 --> 00:06:13.230
And, in particular,
each instance

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:14.850
can be sublinear in size.

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:19.750
And that would be what I want
to highlight, the main result.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:22.630
And this is not the end
of this field in the sense

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:25.570
that there are some also
exciting open problems.

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:28.810
Namely, can you
do deterministic.

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:31.150
So the previous optimal
require randomness.

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:34.690
So the main open
question is that can you

00:06:34.690 --> 00:06:36.490
do randomized
algorithm, can you find

00:06:36.490 --> 00:06:38.240
an algorithm that
solves the connectivity

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:41.800
without a randomization?

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:44.470
Can you solve the weighted
version of vertex connectivity?

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:47.360
So there are a lot of open
problems to be solved.

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:47.980
Thank you.

00:06:47.980 --> 00:06:48.850
I will stop here.

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:51.828
[APPLAUSE]

00:06:51.828 --> 00:06:53.620
NIKHIL SRIVASTAVA:
Thanks a lot, Sorrachai.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:55.120
We have time for
one question maybe.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:01.120
OK, well, so thank you.

